# Real-time / WebSocket Rules

## Architecture
- **Singleton service per endpoint.** One module owns the connection.
- Components subscribe via hooks (`useChannel`, `useGameTick`); never `new WebSocket()` in a component.
- Three layers: Transport (connect/reconnect/heartbeat) → Protocol (zod-validated decode) → Bridge (Redux dispatch).

## Connection lifecycle
- Connect lazily on first subscriber, eagerly on mount for game UIs.
- Heartbeat every 15–30 s; 2 missed pongs = disconnected.
- Reconnect with exponential backoff + jitter (1, 2, 5, 10 s, capped at 30 s).
- Resume cleanly with sequence id / resume token; server replays missed frames.
- Surface a "reconnecting" banner after N attempts; "offline" after the cap.

## Back-pressure
- High-rate streams (>10 Hz): aggregate frames, flush via `requestAnimationFrame`.
- Buffer threshold (e.g. 200 frames): drop oldest non-critical; never silently drop ordered messages.
- Watch `bufferedAmount` on outbound — throttle if it grows.

## Consistency
- Sequence ids on every relevant frame. Drop or reorder out-of-order.
- Periodic snapshots for state that must converge (scoreboards, balances).
- On reconnect without resume support: invalidate dependent caches, refetch.

## Errors & defensive UI
- Validate every inbound frame with zod. Bad frame logs + drops, never crashes connection.
- Components render: `idle` / `connecting` / `live` / `reconnecting` / `offline` / `degraded`.
- `aria-live="polite"` on connection status region.

## Hooks
- Return a snapshot of relevant state; unsubscribe on unmount.
- Multiple subscribers to same channel share one socket.

## Security
- `wss://` in production. Auth via URL param or first message — never cookies (CSRF surface).
- Strip PII from client-side frame logs.
